                <h2>Aggregates and Grouped Reporting</h2>
                <p>Build reporting queries that summarize many rows into decision-making information.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <details class="sql-syntax-ref">
            <summary><i class="fa-solid fa-code"></i> Reference pattern — COUNT, AVG, SUM, MAX, MIN, GROUP BY, HAVING</summary>
            <div class="sql-syntax-body">
                <pre><code>-- Count rows in each group
SELECT group_column, COUNT(*) AS row_count
FROM table_name
GROUP BY group_column;

-- Average a numeric value in each group
SELECT group_column, AVG(score_column) AS average_score
FROM table_name
GROUP BY group_column;

-- Total a numeric value in each group
SELECT category_column, SUM(number_column) AS total_value
FROM table_name
GROUP BY category_column;

-- Filter grouped results after aggregation
SELECT group_column, COUNT(*) AS row_count
FROM table_name
GROUP BY group_column
HAVING COUNT(*) >= 4;</code></pre>
                <p><code>COUNT</code>, <code>AVG</code>, <code>SUM</code>, <code>MAX</code>, and <code>MIN</code> work on grouped data. <code>HAVING</code> is the clause that filters those grouped summaries.</p>
            </div>
        </details>

